-- title:
--   Finite Hopf algebras: kernels of presentations are locally N-generated
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be a field and $B$ a commutative ring carrying a Hopf algebra structure over $k$ which is finite as a $k$-module, both in the same universe. Let $N$ be a natural number and let $\varphi : k[X_0,\dots,X_{N-1}] =$ `MvPolynomial (Fin N) k` $\to B$ be a surjective $k$-algebra homomorphism, and let $\mathfrak n$ be a maximal ideal of `MvPolynomial (Fin N) k` containing $\ker \varphi$. The assertion is that there exists a family $g : \mathrm{Fin}\,N \to k[X_0,\dots,X_{N-1}]$ of $N$ polynomials, each lying in $\ker\varphi$, such that the image of $\ker\varphi$ in the localisation `Localization.AtPrime 𝔫` under the structure map is the ideal generated by the range of the images of the $g_i$ there. Thus the kernel of an arbitrary $N$-variable presentation of $B$ becomes, after localisation at any maximal ideal of the polynomial ring above it, generated by exactly $N$ elements, and moreover by $N$ elements that already lie in the kernel itself.
--
--   This is the statement that a finite commutative group scheme over a field is a local complete intersection, expressed in terms of presentations: the kernel of any presentation in $N$ variables is locally generated by $N$ of its own elements. It is used in the construction of square presentations for the finite levels of $p$-divisible groups over rings of integers, via [`PDivisibleGroup.exists_square_presentation_level_of_ringOfIntegers`](thm.html#PDivisibleGroup.exists_square_presentation_level_of_ringOfIntegers).
